## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-time ingestion, durability, and privacy controls; candidates should propose idempotency keys, deduplication, and verifiable batch digests for regulators without exposing raw data.

## Key Concepts
- At-least-once delivery
- Idempotent processing
- Append-only ledger
- Verifiable batch digests (Merkle roots)
- Regulator verification without raw inputs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dedupe and write to ledger
function ingest(delta, store){
  if (store.has(delta.delta_id)) return false;
  store.set(delta.delta_id, delta);
  // append to append-only ledger
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement Merkle root verification per batch for regulators?
- What tests would you add to simulate network partitions and verify idempotency?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests integration of privacy-preserving computation with hardware-assisted trust, and designs that scale across untrusted fog nodes while preserving tenant isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Threshold cryptography
- Trusted Execution Environments
- Secure aggregation
- Provenance and replay protection
- Cross-tenant isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal handler sketch for /cca_score/tt_share
async function handleShare(req){
  const {tenant_id, device_id, t_of_n, shard, attestation, nonce} = req.body;
  if(!verifyAttestation(attestation, device_id)) throw new Error("Bad attestation");
  // store share and nonce for later aggregation
  await storeShare(tenant_id, device_id, t_of_n, shard, nonce);
}
```

